The check of Paul Zimmermann's tables where each line gives its own k and l.

A line is

  x0 N k l B_00 .. B_0(l-1) B_10 .. B_(k-1)(l-1)

x0 a double in hexadecimal (%la), N, k and l in decimal, 1 <= k <= 13 and
1 <= l <= 8, then the k l words of the B_i, each B_i as l words of 64
bits, the least significant first (the search program is
/home/thery/claudeExp/htr.c).  The lines are half-open: a line is searched
at x0 + j u for j = 0 .. N-1, so ExpCheck.v has closed = false.  The
window is ERR = 0x600000 on the top word of B_0, so for a line of l words
E = 0x600000 * 2^(64 (l - 1)); the enclosures of exp are computed with
64 l + 53 + 64 bits.

The parameters that are the same for every line (beta, kmax = 13,
lmax = 8, m = 43, ERR, guard, closed) are at the top of ExpCheck.v.
line_ok S0 ex n k l B states the six conditions; the sixth is
1 <= k <= 13, 1 <= l <= 8 and 2 E < beta^l.

sample.tar.xz holds the first 5 lines of 13 files of the archive, all the
pairs (k, l) the archive has:

  in0-40 (2,2)  in0-20 (3,2)  in0-10 (4,3)  in0-4 (5,4)  in0-0 (6,4)
  in2 (7,5)  in-10 (8,5)  in24 (9,6)  in100 (10,7)  in-185 (11,7)
  in370 (12,8)  in1024 (13,8)  in-1074 (13,8)

  mkdir -p sample; tar xJf sample.tar.xz -C sample
  ./mksample.sh sample/in*
  make -j1 sample
  make -j1 sample-all    # SampleAll.v: the theorem, one lemma per file
                         # AllCover.v: the coverage, one theorem per run
  make -j1 test          # ExpMutate.v: the bit-flip test on MutData.v

A line (x0, n, k, l, B) holds the inputs x0 + j u, j = 0 .. n - d, where
d is set by the parameter closed of ExpCheck.v: closed = true (d = 0) for
the closed range [x0, x0 + n u], closed = false (d = 1) for the half-open
range [x0, x0 + n u).  Its last input is xl = x0 + (n - d) u.

mksample.sh also writes Cover_N.v, the check (ExpCover.contig, parsing
only) that the lines of a file follow each other: each line starts at the
double right after the last input xl of the previous one, also when x
crosses a binade.  It orders the files by their first x0 and cuts them
into maximal runs of files that follow each other (ExpCover.joins); it
reports on stderr the files that do not join, and leaves out of the runs
the files whose lines do not follow each other.  AllCover.v has one
theorem coverK per run: every normal double y = S 2^(ex - 52) between the
first x0 and the last xl of the run is x0 + j u, 0 <= j <= n - d, in a
line whose text satisfies the six conditions.  The sample files are cut,
so each one is a run of its own.

The whole archive (2203 files, 2931305 lines, one file per binade) is
Paul's all.tar.bz2.  It is in archive/, as thirteen parts
tables0..12.tar.bz2 (under 100 MB each, the size GitHub accepts), with
their SHA256SUMS; INDEX lists the part holding each file.  Then

  ulimit -s unlimited
  ./fullrun.sh 20 2>&1 | tee full.log

checks the parts, unpacks them into tables/, writes one Data_N.v, Run_N.v
and Cover_N.v per file, runs them with 20 workers and builds SampleAll.v
and AllCover.v over all the files.  ./fullrun.sh 20 ARCHIVE takes the
files from the single archive ARCHIVE instead.
